Subject: Calculating p in Minkowsi Distance Formula
Message-ID: <200409131746.i8DHkrD30885@smtp2.ucalgary.ca>

Hi All,

I know its a basic question but I am unable to solve
the Minkowski Distance formula for p:

d = [(x1-x2)^p + (y1-y2)^p]^1/p

I have D, x1, x2, y1, y2 for about 1300 observations,
I want to know the value of p. When I take Logrithms,
p cancels out.

Any suggestion?

Rizwan

From Nikhil.S.Padhye at uth.tmc.edu  Mon Sep 13 19:46:55 2004
From: Nikhil.S.Padhye at uth.tmc.edu (Nikhil S Padhye)
Date: Mon, 13 Sep 2004 14:46:55 -0500
Subject: Re: Calculating p in Minkowsi Distance Formula
Message-ID: <F567BEA12C734C4FA3DC39691BB73869C4F464@UTHEVS3.mail.uthouston.edu>

The p's do not cancel, but there is no closed form expression for p, as
far as I can see. What you have is a nonlinear regression problem to
determine the parameter p:

Log(d) = (1/p) . Log[(x1-x2)^p + (y1-y2)^p]

(Note that log(A+B) is not equal to log(A) + log(B) - the most likely
error you made that led to canceling of p.)

You may want to try:

nl.st <- c(p=2) ## look for solutions near p=2, the usual physical
metric.
mink.nl <- nls(formula, start=nl.st, trace=T)

Where formula is the above formula expressed appropriately in S-Plus
format.

Hope that helps.

Nikhil
